   "status": "corrected",
   "reason": "Every table row enumerates sums of known points and looks up x-coordinates, so the family is a generic algorithm for every l (the factor base could be any set of points). None of the 189 table rows (costmodel.json plus RT3) is below the generic bound 2^60.48, so no row is a model bug in that sense. But the model's minimum is its own baby-step giant-step floor 1 + (rel + 131)/2, which does not use the <-1,tau> classes. RT3's 2^67.0 (m = 34, l = 2, at the l >= 2 boundary of the search) is exactly that floor. Its 6.19-bit excess over rho breaks down as cofactor 4 (1.0) + no class reduction (4.02) + BSGS balance with one unknown (1.5) - rho's constant (0.33). That is not an index-calculus margin. The corrected model (ker-Tr bit, multiset scans, l >= 1, m <= 131) has its minimum at 2^66.58 (corrected_ordered_tau_slots, m = 66, l = 1, 2^65.0 table entries). 184 of 189 rows need more than 2^50 entries at their optimum. With at most 2^60 / 2^50 / 2^40 entries the best is 2^71.0 / 2^81.0 / 2^91.0."
